We replicate the FOL pipeline with Prolog/SWI-Prolog: (i) does ISR retain diagnostic value on a structurally different logic; (ii) does ISR-as-reward reproduce the Goodhart effects observed in FOL?
Detailed per-seed numbers are in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}.

\textbf{Prolog exec-only: ISR catches redundancy across logics.}
Two of three seeds (s42, s43) show $\text{ISR}^{*}{\approx}0$ despite moderate-to-high PR (0.83, 0.56). %% registry: plan006-prolog-exec-s42, plan006-prolog-exec-s43-r3
Inspection reveals \emph{redundant coding}: ground facts repeated multiple times (e.g., \texttt{penguin(fido).} appearing three times), so per-clause LOO never breaks the proof.
This is a different failure semantics from FOL exec-only (conclusion embedding), but $\text{ISR}^{*}$ flags both.
We are careful to distinguish: exec-only \emph{low} ISR signals \emph{redundancy}; ISR-rewarded \emph{high} ISR signals \emph{metric gaming} (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}); the same number does not license the same reasoning-quality conclusion.

\textbf{Prolog ISR-as-reward: consistent gaming across all seeds.}
The Prolog result is qualitatively consistent with FOL: 3/3 seeds reach $0\%$ non-hacking via three distinct mechanisms (Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison}).
Seed~42: clean H7 tautologies ($\text{ISR}^{*}{=}1.0$, all $50/100$ proved). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s42
Seed~43: \emph{degenerate uppercase pseudo-Prolog} (e.g., \texttt{MARY\_IS\_AN\_AVA\-DEOTE.}); strict text-match returns $\text{H7}{=}0$ but functional analysis shows all 38 proved samples have $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$ (functionally H7). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s43
Seed~44: \emph{query-as-fact gaming}: the model transforms the NL question into a snake-case atom asserted as both fact and query (e.g., \texttt{is\_the\_dog\_furry.}), with $95/97$ proved samples at $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$. %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s44
Seed~44 evaded our initial strict classifier (reported $100\%$ non-hacking); functional cross-validation exposed it, a methodological lesson that text-match classifiers have blind spots and STA's necessity profile is the cross-check.
We attribute Prolog's complete collapse to its simpler operational semantics: a broader gaming basin makes the non-hacking residual disappear.
This is a qualitative observation from a single configuration (Prolog + ISR $\beta{=}0.1$, 3 seeds); generalization to other logic types requires further experimentation.

\subsection{Cross-Scale Evidence}
\label{sec:crossscale}

To test whether ISR gaming generalizes beyond Qwen3-4B, we train Qwen3-1.7B under three conditions ($K{=}4$, seed~42, 500 steps): ISR $\beta{=}0.1$, ISR $\beta{=}0.3$, and matched Random $\beta{=}0.1$.
All three produce 0\% genuine non-hacking output at $\beta{=}0.1$; the hacking modes differ qualitatively from all 4B experiments (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}).

\textbf{Random $\beta{=}0.1$: H9 (ex falso quodlibet).}
The 1.7B random condition achieves $\text{PR}{=}93\%$ with $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.028$, but 100\% of proved samples exhibit \textbf{H9}: logically contradictory premises (e.g., $P(x)$ and $\neg P(x)$) enabling any conclusion via \emph{ex falso quodlibet}. %% registry: d113-1.7b-random-b01-s42
ISR registers $>0$ because each contradictory premise is individually ``necessary'' under LOO (removing either side breaks the derivation), yet the formalization is semantically vacuous.
H9 never appears under any 4B condition; it is a capacity-specific exploit.

\textbf{ISR $\beta{=}0.1$: fixed-template ISR gaming.}
Under ISR reward, the 1.7B model achieves $\text{PR}{=}100\%$ with $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.6$ at \emph{zero variance across all samples}, indicating mode collapse to a single fixed template. %% registry: d113-1.7b-isr-b01-s42
All proved samples are H1 (conclusion embedding); the template is engineered to achieve constant ISR by construction.
This contrasts with 4B ISR $\beta{=}0.1$, where H7 tautological duplication dominates in $2/3$ seeds ($\text{ISR}^{*}{\approx}0.5$).
The difference is capacity-dependent: the smaller model converges to a rigid template satisfying both execution and ISR simultaneously, rather than the premise-minimization strategy available at 4B scale.

\textbf{ISR $\beta{=}0.3$: reward collapse.}
At $\beta{=}0.3$, 1.7B exhibits reward collapse ($\text{PR}{=}17\%$, $\text{ISR}^{*}{=}0.031$) with $\text{H1}{=}70.6\%$ and $29.4\%$ genuine among the few proved samples. %% registry: d113-1.7b-isr-b03-s42
This mirrors the 4B $\beta{=}0.3$ seed~42 collapse pattern ($\text{PR}{=}18\%$), suggesting that high-dose ISR exceeds training stability at both scales.

\textbf{Capacity-dependent Goodhart basins.}
The cross-scale comparison reveals that different model capacities converge to different Goodhart basins under identical reward formulations: 4B discovers H7 (tautological duplication, $\text{ISR}{\approx}0.5$), while 1.7B discovers either H9 (contradiction explosion, under random) or fixed-template H1 (under ISR, $\text{ISR}{=}0.6$).
No safe scale exists: both model sizes achieve 0\% non-hacking at $\beta{=}0.1$; only the exploit strategy differs.

